<p>Spain and Brazil demanded that Israel release two activists taken to Israel for questioning after Israeli forces intercepted a Gaza-bound flotilla in international waters near Greece this week. Israel’s Foreign Ministry said Saif Abu Keshek, a Spanish-Swedish national of Palestinian origin, and Brazilian activist Thiago Avila had arrived in Israel and would be transferred to law enforcement authorities. The flotilla included more than 50 vessels that had sailed from ports in France, Spain and Italy. Israel said it took about 175 activists off the vessels, while organizers said 211 people were taken; most participants were transferred to Crete. Israel said the two were connected to the U.S.-sanctioned Popular Conference for Palestinians Abroad. Madrid and Brasília disputed their transfer to Israel and called for them to be allowed to return home.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/09kmP2z86I8ZWVqohGuUSMnrVfOVaHsSMntx1RgEl6ijmZbqAqQ7LO1Oxa69hU0WG7eR8AuZnPN1G08PAqIEx0pk4hWMNapMcSTlnn_rUjbI' alt='Activists of the Global Sumud Flotilla prepare to board a bus at the port of Atherinolakkos on Crete after the flotilla was intercepted by the Israeli Navy in international waters.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Hunger strike claim: ABC News reported that the aid group said the two detainees had begun a hunger strike.</li><li>Released Australians: The Guardian reported that three of six released Australian activists alleged mistreatment while they were held for two days on board an Israeli vessel.</li><li>Remaining vessels: A source said 47 boats were still sailing off southern Crete after 22 vessels were seized, and that the remaining boats planned to anchor before continuing toward Gaza.</li><li>French participants: Le Dauphiné reported that 15 French nationals were among the activists arrested from about 20 boats in the flotilla.</li></ul><blockquote>We are facing an illegal detention in international waters, outside any jurisdiction of the Israeli authorities - José Manuel Albares</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Spanish government: Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez described the Spanish detainee as “abducted” and called on Netanyahu to free him, while saying Spain would protect its citizens. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/video/newsfeed/2026/5/2/spains-sanchez-demands-netanyahu-free-spaniard-seized-on-aid-flotilla'>Al Jazeera</a>, <a href='https://www.sabado.pt/video/detalhe/pedro-sanchez-acusa-israel-e-netanyahu-de-sequestro-ilegal-de-ativistas-da-flotilha-que-ia-para-gaza'>Sábado</a>)</li><li>Israeli Foreign Ministry: The ministry called the voyage a provocation and described the flotilla organizers as “professional provocateurs.” (<a href='https://www.brecorder.com/news/40419246/gaza-flotilla-activists-taken-to-crete-after-israeli-interception'>Business Recorder</a>)</li><li>Global Sumud Flotilla organizers: The group demanded that New Zealand’s government intervene after the interception, saying it should act to uphold international law. (<a href='https://www.odt.co.nz/news/national/flotilla-calls-nz-govt-intervene-after-israeli-interception-rnz'>Otago Daily Times</a>)</li><li>Francesca Albanese, UN special rapporteur: Albanese condemned the interception on X and called for the release of Abu Keshek and Avila, saying the seizure of boats and people in European waters was a crime. (<a href='https://www.middleeasteye.net/live-blog/live-blog-update/aparthied-israel-un-rapporteur-calls-release-gaza-flotilla-activists'>Middle East Eye</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/activists-detention-deepens-spain-israel-tensions-aid-group-132603764'>Activists’ detention deepens Spain-Israel tensions as aid group says men have begun hunger strike</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/05/israel-quizzes-two-gaza-flotilla-activists-angering-spain'>Israel quizzes two Gaza flotilla activists, angering Spain</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li